    "id": 5,
    "topic": "SBIR/STTR as Biotech Launchpad",
    "content_sourced": "NIH's <a href='https://seed.nih.gov/'>SBIR/STTR program</a> has funded early stages of companies including Genzyme, Amgen, and Geron. <a href='https://www.sbir.gov/about'>Phase I grants ($275K)</a> and Phase II ($1M+) provide non-dilutive funding that academic founders use to de-risk before VC rounds. The <a href='https://www.sbir.gov/awards/annual-reports'>program database</a> shows thousands of biotech awards.",
    "sources": [
      {"name": "NIH SEED (SBIR/STTR)", "url": "https://seed.nih.gov/", "type": "government"},
      {"name": "SBIR.gov - About", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/about", "type": "government"},
      {"name": "SBIR Awards Database", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/annual-reports", "type": "government"}
    ],
    "significance": "major"
  },
  {
    "id": 17,
    "topic": "HHMI's Long-Term Bets",
    "content_sourced": "<a href='https://www.hhmi.org/'>Howard Hughes Medical Institute</a> funds investigators, not projects—<a href='https://www.hhmi.org/news/hhmi-announces-largest-ever-competition-investigators'>$200M+/year</a> to researchers with freedom to pursue risky science. HHMI alumni have founded dozens of biotechs. <a href='https://www.hhmi.org/scientists/jennifer-doudna'>Jennifer Doudna</a>, <a href='https://www.hhmi.org/scientists/david-r-liu'>David Liu</a> (base editing), and <a href='https://www.hhmi.org/scientists/feng-zhang'>Feng Zhang</a> were all HHMI investigators.",
    "sources": [
      {"name": "HHMI", "url": "https://www.hhmi.org/", "type": "institutional"},
